The old driver retains guard history in memory until every selected cell finishes,
and captures the whole child output before saving a successful cell. An interrupted
run can therefore lose guard history, failed output, or both. A129 keeps the same
frozen executable and schedule but persists evidence as it becomes available:

- a new, exclusive run directory with an immutable plan and a flushed/fsynced
  event journal; every guard sample is saved, including rejected samples;
- public scene/schedule bytes, a per-cell plan and pre-cell guard before launch;
- child stdout/stderr directly in files; PID, exit/exception and hashes are saved
  after the child is reaped, including failures and timeouts;
- post-cell guard and per-cell driver metadata before A128 validation;
- a terminal result and a separate 21-cell completeness/guard report. A valid
  selected subset remains incomplete evidence for a final scaling curve.

